1
00:00:08,550 --> 00:00:16,452
Tier two. Company. Umbrella Life Sciences,
Incorporated. Twenty-five thousand Points.

2
00:00:16,472 --> 00:00:24,374
Balance, sixty-two thousand. Territory tab
unlocked. The Company banner is available.

3
00:00:24,394 --> 00:00:28,569
Two hundred and eleven new
items in the Shop.

4
00:00:29,158 --> 00:00:34,943
Eight months. From a chair in an empty office to the
second tier of six, in eight months,

5
00:00:34,963 --> 00:00:40,682
with a balance of sixty-two thousand Points and a
company that employed one thousand six

6
00:00:40,702 --> 00:00:46,355
hundred and eleven people, three hundred and
twenty-three of whom had been manufactured

7
00:00:46,375 --> 00:00:47,268
on a Saturday.

8
00:00:47,668 --> 00:00:53,410
He read the new tabs for two hours. Territory, which
was locked at a price he could not

9
00:00:53,430 --> 00:00:59,305
yet reach and which made his hands very still when he
read what it contained. Facilities,

10
00:00:59,325 --> 00:01:05,266
which now offered things with words like *secure
estate* and *subsurface complex* in them.

11
00:01:05,286 --> 00:01:09,305
Personnel, which now offered cohorts
rather than individuals.

12
00:01:09,706 --> 00:01:11,046
And the Company banner.

13
00:01:12,197 --> 00:01:20,520
The Company banner. Ten-pull, twenty thousand, with one
Epic or better guaranteed. Legendary

14
00:01:20,540 --> 00:01:25,871
pool at this tier: packages. A place,
and everything in it.

15
00:01:26,460 --> 00:01:28,472
A place, and everything in it.

16
00:01:28,873 --> 00:01:29,683
Yes.

17
00:01:30,082 --> 00:01:30,969
Twenty thousand.

18
00:01:31,367 --> 00:01:32,849
You have sixty-two.

19
00:01:33,251 --> 00:01:39,047
And Albert Wesker, who had emptied his pockets in
September and emptied them again in

20
00:01:39,067 --> 00:01:45,001
March, looked at a balance of sixty-two thousand
Points and did the arithmetic of a man

21
00:01:45,021 --> 00:01:48,490
who has finally worked out what
game he is playing.

22
00:01:49,010 --> 00:01:54,172
Sixty. Three pulls of ten, then a fourth. If the
fourth is junk I will still be the only

23
00:01:54,192 --> 00:01:58,058
man on this planet who can afford to be
disappointed by a miracle.

24
00:01:58,457 --> 00:02:00,308
The first ten took four minutes.

25
00:02:00,707 --> 00:02:06,869
Commons: two research associates, a fleet of
vehicles, a cold-chain shipping contract,

26
00:02:06,889 --> 00:02:13,051
an industrial autoclave. Uncommons: a *Rapid Assay
production licence for the European

27
00:02:13,071 --> 00:02:19,160
Union*, which Pell would later describe as the
single most valuable piece of paper in

28
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:25,557
the building; a cohort of six security officers; a
patent portfolio in protein chemistry.

29
00:02:25,957 --> 00:02:32,575
Rare, in blue: a second Tyrant, T-103, cradled. It
went into B4 beside the first, and

30
00:02:32,595 --> 00:02:36,168
Gus filed the service tag
without being asked.

31
00:02:36,568 --> 00:02:42,917
Rare again: **Ocular Control.** A small thing, and
the only item in three hundred pulls

32
00:02:42,937 --> 00:02:49,286
that made him genuinely grateful — the ability to
keep his eyes ordinary when the virus

33
00:02:49,306 --> 00:02:54,558
wanted otherwise. He had worn the glasses for
eight months for a reason.

34
00:02:54,957 --> 00:03:00,366
The second ten: junk, mostly. The third ten
produced the guaranteed Epic.

35
00:03:01,516 --> 00:03:09,717
Epic. Anti-Serum Blueprint Line. A universal
countermeasure platform for engineered pathogens.

36
00:03:09,737 --> 00:03:13,914
Note: this item exists because
you will need it.

37
00:03:14,504 --> 00:03:15,575
Explain that note.

38
00:03:15,975 --> 00:03:16,725
No.

39
00:03:17,126 --> 00:03:22,656
He asked four more times over the following six
years. The answer did not change.

40
00:03:23,056 --> 00:03:25,295
And then he spent the last
twenty thousand.

41
00:03:25,695 --> 00:03:32,121
Nine commons. Grey, grey, grey — a reagent lot, a
service contract, three technicians,

42
00:03:32,141 --> 00:03:38,717
an office lease in Dover he did not need — and Wesker
was already reaching for the panel

43
00:03:38,737 --> 00:03:41,566
to close it when the room turned gold.

44
00:03:43,156 --> 00:03:46,376
Legendary. The Arklay Estate.

45
00:03:46,966 --> 00:03:53,140
The panel did not scroll. It unfolded, line by line,
and the System took its time, because

46
00:03:53,160 --> 00:03:57,475
the System had a sense of theatre that
it would never admit to.

47
00:03:58,626 --> 00:04:08,302
The Arklay Estate. A residence of sixty-one rooms in the
Allegheny forest of western Pennsylvania.

48
00:04:08,322 --> 00:04:16,910
A subsurface complex of nine levels, B S L four
throughout, with a containment hall and

49
00:04:16,930 --> 00:04:25,617
a cryogenic hall. Dedicated power. Dedicated water. A
private road, a helipad and a rail

50
00:04:25,637 --> 00:04:34,720
spur. A security detachment of twenty-four, Absolute.
Research staff of forty-one, Absolute.

51
00:04:34,740 --> 00:04:43,526
Senior personnel: Doctor William Birkin. Doctor
Annette Birkin. Sealed vault, level nine:

52
00:04:43,546 --> 00:04:45,504
Progenitor. Not yet.

53
00:04:46,754 --> 00:04:53,899
The narrator will now describe the only time in
sixty-five years that Albert Wesker sat

54
00:04:53,919 --> 00:04:54,805
down twice.

55
00:04:55,204 --> 00:05:00,638
He read the last four lines of that panel standing
up. Then he read the two names again.

56
00:05:00,658 --> 00:05:06,092
Then he put one hand on the back of the leather
chair, and turned it, and sat in it, and

57
00:05:06,112 --> 00:05:09,624
did not say anything at all for a
minute and ten seconds.

58
00:05:10,024 --> 00:05:12,884
And the other one, quietly, from the
back of the skull.

59
00:05:13,404 --> 00:05:14,283
Who are they?

60
00:05:14,684 --> 00:05:20,844
And for once — the only time, in the entire record —
he answered honestly and immediately.

61
00:05:21,363 --> 00:05:26,289
They are dead. They died in a city I destroyed, in a
world that is not this one, thirty

62
00:05:26,309 --> 00:05:28,449
years ago and eighteen years from now.

63
00:05:28,848 --> 00:05:29,687
Friends?

64
00:05:30,088 --> 00:05:34,923
No. Nothing so useful. Will was the only mind I
have ever met that I could not simply

65
00:05:34,943 --> 00:05:39,836
out-work. He was a genius, he was a child, and he
betrayed me, and I betrayed him, and

66
00:05:39,856 --> 00:05:44,406
both of us thought we had planned it. Annette
hated me accurately for six years.

67
00:05:44,807 --> 00:05:47,940
And the machine has simply given
them back to you.

68
00:05:48,338 --> 00:05:53,708
No. It has *issued* them to me. That is not the same
thing, and I intend to keep the difference

69
00:05:53,728 --> 00:05:55,807
in front of me for as long as I live.

70
00:05:56,208 --> 00:05:57,858
He flew out at first light.

71
00:05:58,257 --> 00:06:04,861
The helicopter came in over eleven thousand acres of
Pennsylvania pine, over a private

72
00:06:04,881 --> 00:06:12,023
road that the state highway department's map showed as
an unimproved service track maintained

73
00:06:12,043 --> 00:06:17,567
by a timber trust since 1969, and over a gate
with a crimson iris on it.

74
00:06:17,968 --> 00:06:19,088
And then the house.

75
00:06:19,487 --> 00:06:25,194
Grey stone, three wings, sixty-one rooms, a slate
roof steep enough to shed a winter,

76
00:06:25,214 --> 00:06:31,392
and a carriage sweep in front of it with the gravel
raked. Ivy on the west face, established

77
00:06:31,412 --> 00:06:37,590
— twenty years of ivy. A garage with four cars in it.
Smoke from a chimney, because somebody

78
00:06:37,610 --> 00:06:41,498
had lit a fire that morning, because
somebody lived there.

79
00:06:41,898 --> 00:06:47,599
It was not his house from the other world. It was
not the Spencer mansion; it did not

80
00:06:47,619 --> 00:06:53,858
have the staircase, or the water treatment plant hidden
in the hillside, or the thousand-yard

81
00:06:53,878 --> 00:06:59,647
gallery of a dead man's vanity. It was a plain,
enormous, unpretentious stone house in

82
00:06:59,667 --> 00:07:02,137
the wrong forest in the right decade.

83
00:07:02,538 --> 00:07:08,026
And the way he looked at it, from the air, for ninety
seconds, is the only thing in this

84
00:07:08,046 --> 00:07:13,346
account the narrator cannot tell you with
certainty, because his face was turned away

85
00:07:13,366 --> 00:07:16,038
from the only other person in
the aircraft.

86
00:07:16,437 --> 00:07:22,522
William Birkin came down the steps before the rotors
had stopped, in a lab coat he had

87
00:07:22,542 --> 00:07:25,077
slept in, holding a tablet, talking.

88
00:07:25,598 --> 00:07:28,938
Albert. Albert — good, you're here, I need seventeen
minutes and then you can do whatever

89
00:07:28,958 --> 00:07:32,185
it is you came to do. The cryogenic hall's drawing
four per cent over spec and Annette

90
00:07:32,205 --> 00:07:35,469
says it's the chillers but it isn't the chillers,
it's that somebody plumbed the return

91
00:07:35,489 --> 00:07:38,754
on level six through the same riser as the
containment scrubbers, which works, which is

92
00:07:38,774 --> 00:07:42,039
the problem, because it works until it doesn't and
then we lose forty years of stock in

93
00:07:42,059 --> 00:07:42,568
an afternoon —

94
00:07:42,968 --> 00:07:45,538
He stopped, because he had
finally looked up.

95
00:07:46,057 --> 00:07:48,067
You look strange. Are you ill?
You're never ill.

96
00:07:48,468 --> 00:07:54,147
Wesker stood on the gravel in front of a man who had
been dead for eighteen years in a

97
00:07:54,167 --> 00:07:59,118
world that no longer existed, who was talking
to him about a chiller riser.

98
00:07:59,637 --> 00:08:01,602
How long have you worked for me, Will?

99
00:08:02,002 --> 00:08:06,731
Six years. Seven in August. Why? Is this an audit
thing? Annette said there'd be an audit

100
00:08:06,751 --> 00:08:07,052
thing.

101
00:08:07,451 --> 00:08:07,595
No.

102
00:08:07,995 --> 00:08:12,588
Good, because I have seven hundred and forty samples
in level nine that I would rather

103
00:08:12,608 --> 00:08:15,485
not explain to an auditor. Can I
show you the chiller?

104
00:08:15,884 --> 00:08:22,209
And Albert Wesker — who had tested Marguerite's
obedience with a threat to break her finger,

105
00:08:22,229 --> 00:08:28,140
who had priced a man's destruction in a boardroom
eleven days earlier and enjoyed it —

106
00:08:28,160 --> 00:08:28,485
said:

107
00:08:29,004 --> 00:08:30,251
Yes. Show me the chiller.

108
00:08:30,651 --> 00:08:34,441
He spent four hours down there. Not one
of them on the chiller.

109
00:08:34,841 --> 00:08:41,802
Annette Birkin met him at the level-three airlock with
her arms folded and a look he remembered

110
00:08:41,822 --> 00:08:47,020
so precisely that he had to take a
half-second to arrange his own face.

111
00:08:47,539 --> 00:08:49,829
You've never come here unannounced before.

112
00:08:50,230 --> 00:08:50,686
I own it.

113
00:08:51,086 --> 00:08:55,804
You own a great many things you don't visit.
Whatever you've come to take, take it, and

114
00:08:55,824 --> 00:08:57,655
don't let him give you level nine.

115
00:08:58,056 --> 00:08:58,651
Why not?

116
00:08:59,051 --> 00:09:04,406
Because he'll give you anything you ask for, Dr.
Wesker. He always has. Someone in this

117
00:09:04,426 --> 00:09:09,843
house has to say no to you, and it is not going to be
him, and it is certainly not going

118
00:09:09,863 --> 00:09:14,601
to be the forty-one people downstairs who agree
with everything anybody says.

119
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:22,284
The narrator notes that Annette Birkin was, per the
System's own roster, Absolute — incapable

120
00:09:22,304 --> 00:09:25,110
of disobedience, incapable of doubt.

121
00:09:25,510 --> 00:09:29,670
And she was standing in a doorway telling him
to be careful with her husband.

122
00:09:30,071 --> 00:09:36,229
Wesker filed that. He filed it very carefully
indeed, and it is the first crack in the

123
00:09:36,249 --> 00:09:42,120
story of what the Absolute actually are, and it
does not open again for six years.

124
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:43,591
Nine levels.

125
00:09:43,990 --> 00:09:50,040
Containment wings on three and four. A biologics
suite on five that made Division Seven's

126
00:09:50,060 --> 00:09:56,041
B4 look like a school laboratory. On six, an animal
facility with things in it that Voss

127
00:09:56,061 --> 00:09:59,860
would have needed a year of ethics
paperwork to look at.

128
00:10:00,260 --> 00:10:02,681
And on seven, the cryogenic hall.

129
00:10:03,081 --> 00:10:09,152
It was the size of a cathedral nave and it was mostly
empty, which was somehow worse than

130
00:10:09,172 --> 00:10:15,312
full. Four hundred and eleven cradles in ranks, of
which nine were occupied: six by tissue

131
00:10:15,332 --> 00:10:21,130
banks, two by the T-103s he had shipped up that
morning from Wilmington, and one — at

132
00:10:21,150 --> 00:10:27,221
the far end, alone, under a light that was slightly
brighter than the others — by nothing

133
00:10:27,241 --> 00:10:27,701
at all.

134
00:10:28,221 --> 00:10:29,420
What is that cradle for?

135
00:10:29,820 --> 00:10:34,528
I don't know. It was in the drawings. It's plumbed and
powered and it's rated for something

136
00:10:34,548 --> 00:10:36,970
a great deal heavier than a
Tyrant. I've asked.

137
00:10:37,370 --> 00:10:38,255
Who did you ask?

138
00:10:38,655 --> 00:10:39,826
The System. Obviously.

139
00:10:40,775 --> 00:10:46,561
The narrator would like you to understand that in
that moment, in a cold hall a hundred

140
00:10:46,581 --> 00:10:52,300
and forty metres under a Pennsylvania forest, Albert
Wesker experienced something that

141
00:10:52,320 --> 00:10:57,905
he had not experienced since he had opened his eyes
in a leather chair in September.

142
00:10:58,306 --> 00:10:59,896
He experienced surprise.

143
00:11:00,416 --> 00:11:01,882
(very cold) Say that again.

144
00:11:02,282 --> 00:11:06,903
I asked the System. The interface. The — Albert,
why are you looking at me like that?

145
00:11:06,923 --> 00:11:11,600
It's been answering my requisitions since 2021. How
else do you think I've been buying

146
00:11:11,620 --> 00:11:12,091
anything?

147
00:11:12,491 --> 00:11:18,258
He could see it. He could not perceive the Shop or
the Gacha or the roster or the Points

148
00:11:18,278 --> 00:11:24,176
— Wesker established that within about ninety seconds,
with four questions in a flat voice

149
00:11:24,196 --> 00:11:29,897
— but Dr. William Birkin could open a panel, request
equipment, and receive it, and had

150
00:11:29,917 --> 00:11:35,552
been doing so for six years, and had never once
thought it was strange, because nobody

151
00:11:35,572 --> 00:11:38,971
in that house had ever thought
anything was strange.

152
00:11:39,491 --> 00:11:40,494
You are a *terminal*.

153
00:11:40,894 --> 00:11:41,620
That's rude.

154
00:11:42,019 --> 00:11:43,277
It was not an insult.

155
00:11:43,677 --> 00:11:49,474
Later, on the surface, alone, he put the question to
the System directly, and the System

156
00:11:49,494 --> 00:11:54,367
answered it in sixteen words that he turned
over for the next forty years.

157
00:11:54,886 --> 00:11:59,572
Absolute personnel are part of the
estate. You are not.

158
00:11:59,972 --> 00:12:06,217
He stood on the terrace at dusk with the valley below
him full of fog and eleven thousand

159
00:12:06,237 --> 00:12:08,962
acres of pine going black at the edges.

160
00:12:09,363 --> 00:12:15,340
Behind him, through a window, William Birkin was
arguing with a screen about a chiller

161
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:21,407
riser. Below him, a hundred and forty metres down,
four hundred and eleven cradles were

162
00:12:21,427 --> 00:12:27,614
cold, and nine of them were occupied, and one was
waiting for something that had not been

163
00:12:27,634 --> 00:12:28,033
named.

164
00:12:28,432 --> 00:12:34,068
Eight months ago he had woken in a chair with ten
thousand Points, eleven people and a

165
00:12:34,088 --> 00:12:36,633
basement door that did not go anywhere.

166
00:12:37,033 --> 00:12:40,083
The passenger had been
listening. It spoke now.

167
00:12:40,602 --> 00:12:44,517
This is the part where a man says
something sentimental.

168
00:12:44,917 --> 00:12:45,269
Yes.

169
00:12:45,668 --> 00:12:50,014
Then say it. I will not use it
against you today.

170
00:12:50,413 --> 00:12:57,834
And Albert Wesker, Director of Umbrella Life
Sciences, Tier II, looked out over the fog.

171
00:12:58,354 --> 00:13:02,762
Last time, there was a board of directors. There was
an old man in a chateau who thought

172
00:13:02,782 --> 00:13:07,090
he had made me. There were eleven committees, two
governments, a chain of command, and

173
00:13:07,110 --> 00:13:10,966
thirty years of asking permission from men I
could have killed with one hand.

174
00:13:11,245 --> 00:13:15,417
This time there is no Spencer. There is no
board. There is no one above me.

175
00:13:16,886 --> 00:13:25,850
Umbrella. Tier two. Director: Albert Wesker. One
thousand six hundred and five personnel,

176
00:13:25,870 --> 00:13:34,026
eighty-seven of them Absolute. Seven facilities.
No territory. Next tier: Agency.

177
00:13:34,616 --> 00:13:35,947
*Territory: none.*

178
00:13:36,346 --> 00:13:41,644
He read that line, on a terrace above a valley he
owned, in front of a house that had

179
00:13:41,664 --> 00:13:44,397
been given to him with two
ghosts inside it.

180
00:13:44,917 --> 00:13:45,617
Not for long.

181
00:13:46,017 --> 00:13:47,257
End of Part One.
